{"builder":"Goal: add a --tsv flag to the CLI so tab-separated stdin is parsed with a tab separator, while behaviour without the flag stays exactly as it is now (issue https://github.com/o/csv2json/issues/5).\n\nCurrent state: bin/csv2json.js reads stdin and calls parse(readFileSync(0, 'utf8')) and ignores argv. src/parse.js exports parse(text, sep = ','). test/csv.test.js uses node:test and already has a 'cli' test that runs bin/csv2json.js via execFileSync. package.json has \"test\": \"node --test\".\n\nSteps:\n1. In bin/csv2json.js, check whether '--tsv' appears anywhere in process.argv.slice(2). If it does, call parse(text, '\\t'); if not, call parse(text) the same way it is called today. Keep the output format the same: JSON.stringify(result) followed by one '\\n'. Do not change how unknown arguments are handled, and do not add --help, --sep/--delimiter or auto-detection. Do not add quoting rules. Leave src/parse.js alone unless you need to change it, and if you do, don't change how it handles CSV.\n2. In test/csv.test.js (or a new test/*.test.js file that node --test picks up), add tests that run bin/csv2json.js with execFileSync('node', ['bin/csv2json.js', '--tsv'], { input, encoding: 'utf8' }) and use assert.equal to check the exact stdout:\n   - input 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' -> '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\\n'\n   - input 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' -> '[{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]\\n'\n   - input 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' -> '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]\\n'\n   Also add a test that runs without the flag on input 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' and expects the output '[{\"a\\\\tb\":\"1\\\\t2\"}]\\n' (a JSON tab escape inside the key and the value). Keep the existing tests.\n3. In README.md, add a section on the --tsv flag. Say it reads tab-separated input and give the example invocation `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv`. Keep the existing CSV example.\n4. From the repo root, run `npm test` and confirm it exits 0. Then run each of these and compare the output exactly:\n   - printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv; echo \"exit=$?\"  -> [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}] then exit=0\n   - printf 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv  -> [{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]\n   - printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv  -> [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]\n   - printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js  -> [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\n   - printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js  -> [{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}] (literal backslash-t, the JSON escape)\n5. Open one pull request with the CLI change, the tests and the README change.","verifier":"Check the pull request head for issue https://github.com/o/csv2json/issues/5. Run every command from the repository root at the PR head. Run `npm install` first only if package.json now declares dependencies. There is one check per expect line, in order. Compare output byte for byte. To see the trailing newline, pipe the output through `od -c | tail -3` or `| cat -A`.\n\nCheck 1. Expect: \"`printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by a newline and exits 0.\"\nRun: printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv; echo \"exit=$?\"\nPASS: stdout is exactly [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}] followed by a single newline, and then exit=0. FAIL: any other output, a missing trailing newline, or a nonzero exit.\n\nCheck 2. Expect: \"With --tsv, commas inside a field stay literal: `printf 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]` followed by a newline.\"\nRun: printf 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv\nPASS: stdout is exactly [{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}] followed by a newline. FAIL: anything else.\n\nCheck 3. Expect: \"With --tsv and several data rows, every row comes out in order: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]` followed by a newline.\"\nRun: printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv\nPASS: stdout is exactly [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}] followed by a newline. FAIL: anything else, including the rows in a different order.\n\nCheck 4. Expect: \"Without the flag, CSV still works as before: `printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by a newline.\"\nRun: printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js\nPASS: stdout is exactly [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}] followed by a newline. FAIL: anything else.\n\nCheck 5. Expect: \"Without the flag, tabs are not treated as separators: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}]` followed by a newline, which is the current output.\"\nRun: printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js\nPASS: stdout is exactly [{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}] followed by a newline. Here \\t is the two-character JSON escape (a backslash and then t), so the output is one object with one key. FAIL: anything else, for example [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}].\n\nCheck 6. Expect: \"`npm test` exits 0, and test/ contains at least one test that runs bin/csv2json.js with --tsv on tab-separated input and checks its output.\"\nRun: npm test; echo \"exit=$?\"\nThen run: grep -n -- '--tsv' test/*.js and read each matching test.\nPASS: exit=0, and at least one test under test/ runs bin/csv2json.js with the --tsv argument on input that contains tab characters and asserts on the output. FAIL: a nonzero exit, or no such test (for example, the only --tsv tests call parse() directly and never run the CLI, or they run it but make no assertion on its output).\n\nCheck 7. Expect: \"README.md documents the --tsv flag with an example invocation.\"\nRead README.md at the head.\nPASS: README.md mentions the --tsv flag and gives an example invocation that uses it, such as node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv. FAIL: --tsv is not mentioned, or there is no example invocation that uses it.\n\nAdvisory (none of this affects PASS or FAIL):\n- The issue's proof for check 6 names `npm run test:cli`, but package.json defines only a `test` script. Check 6 uses `npm test`, as its expect line says. If the PR adds a test:cli script, you may run it too.\n- Note whether the README says the flag means tab-separated input. Note whether the PR adds --sep, --help, auto-detection or quoting rules, or changes how unknown arguments are handled. All of these are non-goals.\n- Optionally run `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js foo --tsv` to see whether the flag works at any position among the arguments."}